1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,000
Hello.

2
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:03,000
You've launched an agent and watched its loop run.

3
00:00:03,000 --> 00:00:07,000
Now let's talk about how prompts get into that loop.

4
00:00:07,000 --> 00:00:10,000
The Agent SDK takes your input two ways.

5
00:00:10,000 --> 00:00:12,000
You can stream a live series of messages

6
00:00:12,000 --> 00:00:16,000
or send a single string and get one answer back.

7
00:00:16,000 --> 00:00:18,000
That sounds similar,

8
00:00:18,000 --> 00:00:20,000
but they actually behave quite differently,

9
00:00:20,000 --> 00:00:23,000
and this lesson is going to draw that line. Let's dig in.

10
00:00:24,000 --> 00:00:27,000
Let's set the two modes side by side

11
00:00:27,000 --> 00:00:30,000
because the choice shapes what your agent can do.

12
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:32,000
Streaming Input is the first mode.

13
00:00:32,000 --> 00:00:35,000
Your prompt isn't a single string.

14
00:00:35,000 --> 00:00:37,000
It's an async series of messages,

15
00:00:37,000 --> 00:00:40,000
and the agent runs as a persistent, long-lived process

16
00:00:40,000 --> 00:00:42,000
that you feed over time.

17
00:00:42,000 --> 00:00:45,000
This is the default, and it's the recommended one.

18
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:48,000
Single Message Input is the other mode.

19
00:00:48,000 --> 00:00:50,000
Your prompt is just one string,

20
00:00:50,000 --> 00:00:53,000
the agent answers once, and the run is done.

21
00:00:53,000 --> 00:00:55,000
That's a clean fit for one-off requests

22
00:00:55,000 --> 00:00:59,000
or stateless environments, like a serverless function

23
00:00:59,000 --> 00:01:03,000
that spins up fresh for each call, same loop underneath.

24
00:01:03,000 --> 00:01:07,000
What differs is how prompts flow in and what that unlocks.

25
00:01:08,000 --> 00:01:11,000
So why is Streaming Input the recommended default?

26
00:01:11,000 --> 00:01:14,000
Because running the agent as a live process

27
00:01:14,000 --> 00:01:18,000
unlocks things a one-shot string simply can't do.

28
00:01:18,000 --> 00:01:21,000
You can attach images directly in the messages you send,

29
00:01:21,000 --> 00:01:23,000
so vision is on the table.

30
00:01:23,000 --> 00:01:26,000
You can queue new messages while a turn is still running,

31
00:01:26,000 --> 00:01:29,000
feeding the agent more as it works.

32
00:01:29,000 --> 00:01:31,000
You can interrupt a run in real time

33
00:01:31,000 --> 00:01:32,000
through the client object

34
00:01:32,000 --> 00:01:34,000
instead of waiting for it to finish.

35
00:01:34,000 --> 00:01:38,000
And you get natural, multi-turn context,

36
00:01:38,000 --> 00:01:40,000
where the conversation persists

37
00:01:40,000 --> 00:01:42,000
without you stitching it together.

38
00:01:42,000 --> 00:01:44,000
Add it up, and Streaming Input is the mode

39
00:01:44,000 --> 00:01:47,000
built for real, interactive agents.

40
00:01:48,000 --> 00:01:49,000
Let's be fair to Single Message Input,

41
00:01:49,000 --> 00:01:51,000
because it earns its place.

42
00:01:51,000 --> 00:01:54,000
Its shape is a one-shot string.

43
00:01:54,000 --> 00:01:57,000
You hand the agent a prompt, it answers once,

44
00:01:57,000 --> 00:02:00,000
and no live session stays open afterward.

45
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:03,000
That's exactly what you want in a stateless setting,

46
00:02:03,000 --> 00:02:06,000
a serverless function, or a batch job

47
00:02:06,000 --> 00:02:10,000
that spins up, answers, and shuts down for each request.

48
00:02:10,000 --> 00:02:12,000
There's no persistent process to manage,

49
00:02:12,000 --> 00:02:14,000
which keeps deployment simple.

50
00:02:14,000 --> 00:02:18,000
Now, does that mean no memory ever? Not quite.

51
00:02:18,000 --> 00:02:20,000
You can still get multi-turn behavior,

52
00:02:20,000 --> 00:02:23,000
but only by using the Continue and Resume Session options

53
00:02:23,000 --> 00:02:25,000
to reconnect to a prior run.

54
00:02:25,000 --> 00:02:28,000
It's more deliberate than Streaming's natural persistence,

55
00:02:28,000 --> 00:02:32,000
but for one-off work, that trade is usually the right one.

56
00:02:33,000 --> 00:02:35,000
So how do you actually choose?

57
00:02:35,000 --> 00:02:38,000
Match the mode to the shape of your workload.

58
00:02:38,000 --> 00:02:40,000
If you're building something interactive,

59
00:02:40,000 --> 00:02:44,000
tool-heavy, or multi-turn, reach for Streaming Input.

60
00:02:44,000 --> 00:02:47,000
It's built for agents that carry a conversation

61
00:02:47,000 --> 00:02:48,000
and act as they go.

62
00:02:48,000 --> 00:02:50,000
If you just need a single answer,

63
00:02:50,000 --> 00:02:52,000
or you're deploying into a stateless function

64
00:02:52,000 --> 00:02:55,000
that wakes up per request,

65
00:02:55,000 --> 00:02:58,000
single message input is the cleaner fit.

66
00:02:58,000 --> 00:02:59,000
And here's a quick test.

67
00:02:59,000 --> 00:03:01,000
Do you need to attach images

68
00:03:01,000 --> 00:03:04,000
or interrupt a run while it's live?

69
00:03:04,000 --> 00:03:06,000
Both of those point straight at Streaming.

70
00:03:06,000 --> 00:03:09,000
When you're genuinely unsure, lean on the default.

71
00:03:09,000 --> 00:03:12,000
Streaming Input is the recommended mode,

72
00:03:12,000 --> 00:03:15,000
so starting there rarely puts you in the wrong place.

73
00:03:16,000 --> 00:03:18,000
This is the spot where people get tangled,

74
00:03:18,000 --> 00:03:20,000
so let's clear it up directly.

75
00:03:20,000 --> 00:03:24,000
First, Streaming and Single Message are input modes

76
00:03:24,000 --> 00:03:26,000
into one shared agent loop.

77
00:03:26,000 --> 00:03:28,000
They aren't separate engines.

78
00:03:28,000 --> 00:03:32,000
Second, Streaming Input doesn't require the client object.

79
00:03:32,000 --> 00:03:35,000
Query itself accepts an async prompt,

80
00:03:35,000 --> 00:03:38,000
so you can stream input straight through Query.

81
00:03:39,000 --> 00:03:41,000
Interrupts are the one exception.

82
00:03:41,000 --> 00:03:45,000
They still require Clawed SDK Client,

83
00:03:45,000 --> 00:03:46,000
even in streaming mode.

84
00:03:46,000 --> 00:03:51,000
Third, keep SDK Input separate from API Output Streaming.

85
00:03:51,000 --> 00:03:54,000
The stream true flag on a raw model call

86
00:03:54,000 --> 00:03:56,000
is a different mechanism entirely.

87
00:03:56,000 --> 00:04:00,000
And fourth, if you want live output as it's generated,

88
00:04:00,000 --> 00:04:03,000
turn on Include Partial Messages.

89
00:04:03,000 --> 00:04:05,000
That gives you stream event deltas,

90
00:04:05,000 --> 00:04:08,000
token by token, as the agent writes.

91
00:04:08,000 --> 00:04:09,000
Sort those apart,

92
00:04:09,000 --> 00:04:12,000
and the whole streaming picture snaps into focus.

93
00:04:13,000 --> 00:04:16,000
Keep this slide handy as your side-by-side reminder,

94
00:04:16,000 --> 00:04:19,000
because two different ideas share the word streaming.

95
00:04:19,000 --> 00:04:21,000
Input streaming is about sending.

96
00:04:21,000 --> 00:04:24,000
It's how you feed prompts into the loop

97
00:04:24,000 --> 00:04:26,000
as an async series of messages.

98
00:04:26,000 --> 00:04:28,000
Output streaming is about receiving.

99
00:04:28,000 --> 00:04:32,000
It's watching the model's tokens arrive as they generate

100
00:04:32,000 --> 00:04:34,000
instead of waiting for the finished response.

101
00:04:34,000 --> 00:04:38,000
In Python, you turn that on with Include Partial Messages,

102
00:04:38,000 --> 00:04:41,000
which delivers live deltas while the agent writes.

103
00:04:41,000 --> 00:04:44,000
Here's the part that trips people up.

104
00:04:44,000 --> 00:04:47,000
The two are independent choices you can mix freely.

105
00:04:47,000 --> 00:04:50,000
You can stream input without streaming output,

106
00:04:50,000 --> 00:04:53,000
and you can stream output on a single message run.

107
00:04:53,000 --> 00:04:55,000
Picture a chat UI.

108
00:04:55,000 --> 00:04:58,000
Streaming input carries the user's messages in,

109
00:04:58,000 --> 00:05:00,000
and output streaming paints the reply

110
00:05:00,000 --> 00:05:03,000
on-screen token by token.

111
00:05:03,000 --> 00:05:04,000
With the directions clear,

112
00:05:04,000 --> 00:05:06,000
let's lock down the exact vocabulary.

113
00:05:07,000 --> 00:05:09,000
Let's lock the vocabulary,

114
00:05:09,000 --> 00:05:12,000
because these three terms get swapped constantly.

115
00:05:12,000 --> 00:05:14,000
Streaming input means feeding the loop

116
00:05:14,000 --> 00:05:16,000
an async series of messages.

117
00:05:16,000 --> 00:05:20,000
It's the default input mode and the recommended one.

118
00:05:20,000 --> 00:05:23,000
Include Partial Messages is a different lever.

119
00:05:23,000 --> 00:05:26,000
It's the Python flag that turns on live output,

120
00:05:26,000 --> 00:05:28,000
delivering the model's tokens to you

121
00:05:28,000 --> 00:05:31,000
as stream event messages while it writes.

122
00:05:31,000 --> 00:05:33,000
And stream true is different again.

123
00:05:33,000 --> 00:05:37,000
That's the API-level response flag on a raw model call,

124
00:05:37,000 --> 00:05:41,000
and it has nothing to do with the SDK's input modes.

125
00:05:41,000 --> 00:05:42,000
Notice the pattern.

126
00:05:42,000 --> 00:05:45,000
One term is about how prompts go in,

127
00:05:45,000 --> 00:05:49,000
one is about watching output come out through the SDK,

128
00:05:49,000 --> 00:05:51,000
and one belongs to the raw API.

129
00:05:51,000 --> 00:05:53,000
Same word, three jobs.

130
00:05:54,000 --> 00:05:56,000
Let's turn all of this into a default

131
00:05:56,000 --> 00:05:58,000
you can actually reach for.

132
00:05:58,000 --> 00:06:02,000
For most agents, you build startWithStreamingInput.

133
00:06:02,000 --> 00:06:03,000
It's the recommended mode,

134
00:06:03,000 --> 00:06:06,000
and it keeps the full toolbox open.

135
00:06:06,000 --> 00:06:08,000
Images, interrupts, queued messages,

136
00:06:08,000 --> 00:06:11,000
and natural multi-turn context

137
00:06:11,000 --> 00:06:14,000
only drop to single message input

138
00:06:14,000 --> 00:06:16,000
when you have a real reason,

139
00:06:16,000 --> 00:06:18,000
usually a stateless one-off job

140
00:06:18,000 --> 00:06:21,000
where a persistent process would just be overhead.

141
00:06:21,000 --> 00:06:24,000
Layer output streaming on top whenever a human

142
00:06:24,000 --> 00:06:28,000
is watching responses appear, and you want them live.

143
00:06:28,000 --> 00:06:30,000
The through line is simple.

144
00:06:30,000 --> 00:06:32,000
Begin from the default,

145
00:06:32,000 --> 00:06:33,000
understand what each mode gives up,

146
00:06:34,000 --> 00:06:37,000
and switch deliberately when the workload asks for it.

147
00:06:37,000 --> 00:06:41,000
That way, your input mode is a decision, not an accident.

148
00:06:42,000 --> 00:06:44,000
Let's go over the bottom line.

149
00:06:44,000 --> 00:06:47,000
Streaming input and single message sound alike,

150
00:06:47,000 --> 00:06:50,000
but they pull in different directions.

151
00:06:50,000 --> 00:06:53,000
Streaming input runs a live process with images,

152
00:06:53,000 --> 00:06:57,000
interrupts, and natural multi-turn, and it's the default.

153
00:06:57,000 --> 00:06:59,000
Single message input is a single string

154
00:06:59,000 --> 00:07:01,000
for stateless one-off work.

155
00:07:01,000 --> 00:07:03,000
Both feed the same shared loop,

156
00:07:04,000 --> 00:07:07,000
so you're choosing capability, not a different engine.

157
00:07:07,000 --> 00:07:10,000
That wraps up streaming versus single message input.

158
00:07:10,000 --> 00:07:11,000
Join me in the next video.

